Syntax overview, by MC_8 - Carl M. Gregory <mc8@purehype.net>
    ________________________________________________ ________ ____ __


Commands
--------
  search <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>>*
    - Searches the array name' of all responds setup.

  rsearch <response>*
    - Searches the responses of all responds setup.

  info <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>>
    - Gives the response of the given array name'.

  add <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to> [global_flag(s)"|"[channel_flag(s)] [channel]]> <response>
    - Adds a respond record.

  addor <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>> <response>
    - Adds an "or" response' to the given array name'.

  addand <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>> <or number> <response>
    - Adds an "and" response' to the given array name'.

  del <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>> 
    - Deletes a respond record.

  delor <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>> <or number>
    - Deletes an "or" response' of a respond record.

  deland <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to>> <or number> <and number>
    - Deletes an "and" response' of a respond record.

  replace <"anti-idle"|<"all"|#channel1[,#channel2[,#channelN]]> <respond+to> [global_flag(s)"|"[channel_flag(s)] [channel]]> <response>
    - This does the same as the add command, except it doesn't care if 
      info already exists or not.  If the respond record already 
      exists, it will overwrite it.  If not, it will make it.

 Foot notes to commands:
   o For <respond+to>, use +'s for spaces.
   o A CTCP is passed via <oct 001><KEYWORD> <Text><oct 001>.  So, to 
     detect something like an action:
       example:  ?ACTION+gets+himself+some+coffee!?


Response commands
-----------------
  /msg <nick/#channel> <text>
    - Sends a private message to <nick/#channel>.

  /notice <nick/#channel> <text>
    - Sends a notice to <nick/#channel>.

  /quote <RAW Server Commands>
    - Performs a raw server command.  For more information on raw 
      server commands check out this wonderful document:  
      http://www.user-com.undernet.org/documents/rfc1459.txt

  /me [nick/#channel] <text>
    - Performs an action in [nick/#channel].  If [nick/#channel] is not 
      specified, it will default to the channel it was triggered in.

  /action [nick/#channel] <text>
    - Performs an action in [nick/#channel].  If [nick/#channel] is not 
      specified, it will default to the channel it was triggered in.

  /loop <respond to>
    - This will have the script loop/ call it's self over with a 
      different <respond+to>.
        Example:  If you setup a respond record to reply to:     :)
                  You might want to add ':>', ':P' .. and so on to 
                  '/loop :)' instead of adding "and" and "or" responses 
                  to each one separately.

 Foot notes to response commands:
   o If no /command is given, it will default to /msg'n the channel it 
     was triggered from.
   o The anti-idle uses the same, except there is no /loop and for 
     /action and /me a nick/#channel must be specified.  If there is no 
     /command, anti-idle will post a message in dcc chat stating it 
     doesn't know what to do.


Response variables
------------------
  %rnick
    - Returns a random nick in the channel it was triggered in.

  %rnick{<token>}
    - Returns a random nick in the channel it was triggered in.  The 
      difference between this and %rnick is best described with an 
      example:
        1) test %rnick test %rnick test %rnick 
             == test MC_8 test MC_8 test MC_8
        2) test %rnick{abc} test %rnick{123} test %rnick{abc}
             == test MC_8 test bassin247 test MC_8
      If you still don't understand, I will try and explain it.  When 
      %rnick's are replaced, it replaces them all at once.  %rnick asks 
      for a random nick, then replaces all %rnicks with that 1 reply.  
      The token is there so you can have more than 1 %rnick, and it not 
      be the same (logically, possible to be the same if 1 person in 
      the channel).

   %nick
     - The IRC Nickname of the person that triggered the response.

   %uhost
     - The ident@hostname of the person that triggered the response.

   %hand
     - The handle of the person that triggered the response.  If the 
       user is unknown by the bot, a * is returned instead.

   %chan
     - The channel in witch the response was triggered in.

   %botnick
     - The bot's IRC Nickname.

 Foot notes to response variables:
   o There are custom variables that are defined in the <respond+to>'s.
       Example: If the <reply+to> equals this:  Hello+%person
                And the response equals this:  Yea, hello %person!
                And someone said this in the channel:  Hello MC_8
                Then the bot would respond with this:  Yea, hello MC_8!
     A little tricky to understand, that's the best I can do.  If you 
     feel you can explain this better, let me know.
 

Glossary
-------
Array name  = "anti-idle" or "<channel> <respond+to>" section.
And response= Respond records are setup on ORs and ANDs.
              A quick example is:
    /msg nickname hi AND /msg nickname What's up? OR /msg #channel test
              First sorted by the or's, there are 2 two choose from.  
              One the proper or is selected by the script then all the 
              ands are performed.
Or response = see And response.



